course: dictionary
lesson: 782
title: Because and its short forms
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Because» tells you the reason something happened. Ask why, and the answer comes after «because»: «I am wet because it rained.» The rain is the reason, and «because» joins the reason to what happened.
explain: Use «because of» before a noun, a thing or an event, not before a full sentence. «The game stopped because of the rain» is correct. «The game stopped because of it rained» is wrong, because «it rained» is a whole sentence, so you need «because» on its own.
explain: Talk and texts often shorten it to «cos», «coz», «'cause» or «bc». These are fine with friends, but use full «because» in stories, science answers and tests. In maths, an answer to «explain why» usually starts with «because».
fact: «Because» gives the reason for something.
fact: «Because of» comes before a noun, such as «because of the rain».
fact: «Cos», «coz», «'cause» and «bc» are casual short forms of «because».
fact: «Because» can also be a refusal to give a reason: «Why?» «Because I said so.»
fact: Formal writing uses «owing to», «due to» or «on account of» where you would say «because of».
exercise: «Vent water can be hotter than three hundred degrees, but it does not boil because of the huge pressure of the deep sea.» What stops the water boiling? | The water is too cold | The vent is closed for repairs | *The huge pressure of the deep sea | The fish swim away
exercise: Which word is a short, casual form of «because»? | however | *bc | although | therefore
exercise: Which sentence uses «because of» correctly? | The game stopped because the rain. | The game stopped because of rain it. | The game stopped because of it rained. | *The game stopped because of the rain.
test: Ice melts «because it gets warm». What is the cause? | It is blue | Someone sang a song | It is night-time | *It gets warm
test: Which phrase is the formal way to say «because of» in a report? | *due to | bc | cos | 'cause
exam: In a formal report, which phrase replaces «because» in: The school closed because of the storm? | cos the storm | *owing to the storm | bc the storm | 'cause the storm
